;;; A pattern inside a pattern whose relation no parameter binds matches
;;; nothing, in silence — regression
;;; `nested-unbound-head` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(box (a b))`, and one rule whose pattern `(box (?r b))` names
;;; the relation of its inner pattern by `?r`, a variable the rule has no
;;; parameter for.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A relation is named by a name or by a parameter its activator binds
;;; (Q29), and `?r` is neither, so such a pattern is refused at load, with a
;;; line in Ein's spelling naming the rule and the clause. Every command that
;;; loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). The same pattern at the top
;;; level, `(?r b)` (`examples/ein-bugs/unbound-relation-head.ein`), is
;;; refused at load with the same line.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; At `32736ac8` (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE`, release
;;; and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same) the program loaded, and
;;; the rule compiler left `(?r b)` an opaque slot (`compile.rs`' `slot`,
;;; `plan::Slot::Opaque`), which unifies with nothing, so `r` never fired:
;;; root held no `hit`, exit 0, with no word of `?r`. The loader refuses it:
;;; *kb load error: rule 'r': its :match writes (?r
;;; b) with ?r as its relation, which no parameter binds — a relation is
;;; named by a name or by a parameter its activator binds*; the top-level
;;; `(?r b)` gets the same line.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation box Node :why "{?3} is in a box") (relation hit Node :why "{?3} is hit") (relation a Node :why "a holds of {?3}")
(box (a b))
(rule r () :match (box (?r b)) :assert (hit z) :why "some fact about b is in a box, so z is hit")
